  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThis work week continues to look quiet with above normal \ntemperatures as the next chance for precipitation doesn't look to \narrive until this weekend at the earliest. Today, quasi-zonal upper \nlevel flow will prevail over the central CONUS. This coupled with \nmodest WAA will allow highs to push a few degrees above normal in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. Tonight into tomorrow a upper level \ntrough will dig south along the west coast. This will allow \nupstream, upper level ridging to build over the central CONUS. \nHeight rises coupled with continued modest WAA will drive highs into \nthe low to mid 70s. Wednesday the upper level trough will continue to \ndig southward off the California coast further amplifying the ridge \nover the central CONUS. These continued height rise will drive highs \ninto the mid to upper 70s. Thursday the upper level trough off the \nwest coast finally pushes onshore along the California coast and \nmoves into the central Rockies by Friday. This will push the upper \nlevel ridge axis eastward directly over the forecast area for \nThursday and Friday pushing highs 10-15F degrees above normal with \nhighs in the mid 70s to lower 80s. By Saturday, a much weakened \nupper level trough will push northeastward from the central Rockies \ninto the northern Plains. This will force an attendant cold front \ninto the Plains Saturday however, the local area will remain ahead \nthis front under moderate WAA which will keep highs in the mid 70s \nto lower 80s. The cold front will look to sag into the area Saturday \nnight into Sunday as the upper level trough moves into the Upper \nMidwest. Moisture with this front looks meager at this this time \nhowever, some light showers will be possible (20-30%) along and west \nof the I-35 corridor. High pressure looks to quick move into the \narea Sunday behind the front and with no cold air advecting in \nbehind the front, highs will remain above normal in the 70s",
